// @proven: 0
// @failed: 0
// @unknown: 1
// Soundness: a mutation the symbolic walker cannot see must make the value UNKNOWN,
// never leave it at its stale pre-call value.
//
// The walker builds its environment from `Assign` statements. Passing a variable to a
// `&mut` parameter mutates it with no assignment anywhere in the caller, so `x` kept the
// binding `0` across the call to `bump` and this postcondition was reported **proven** —
// for a function that returns 100. Verified by running it: `milo run` prints 100.
//
// Now every name reaching a `&mut`/`*mut` parameter (and every method-call receiver, since
// resolving `&mut self` needs checker tables this pass doesn't have) is havoced at the
// call. `bump` has no contract, so the post-call `x` is a free variable and the verdict is
// UNKNOWN: not proven (the false proof this pins), and not `failed` either, because a
// model over a free variable is not a counterexample anyone can reproduce (see
// `unconstrainedHavocs` in verify.ts). Give bump an `ensures` and the same clause is
// refuted with a real one: tests/prove/builtinPushFrame.milo.
fn bump(n: &mut i64): void {
    n = n + 100
}

fn f(): i64
ensures result == 0
{
    var x: i64 = 0
    bump(x)
    return x
}

fn main() {
    print(f())
}
